 and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6dd26caa40bc8fff3fdfa6bb846a5ffdac481a47.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.6dd26caa40bc8fff3fdfa6bb846a5ffdac481a47.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued October 24 at 8:35PM MDT until October 26 at 1:00PM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Catastrophic flooding caused by excessive rainfall is\nexpected along the San Juan River between Four Corners and Mexican\nHat. This is an emergency situation!\n\n* WHERE...Western Montezuma County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern San Juan County in southeastern Utah...\n\n* WHEN...Until 100 PM MDT Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding along San Juan River is occurring with major\nflooding forecast. Low-water crossing are inundated with water and\nmay not be passable. The river is running fast and high. Do not\nattempt to cross.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 830 PM MDT, Major flooding is expected to occur along the San\nJuan River from Four Corners to Mexican Hat. The San Juan River\nis forecast to crest around midnight tonight at the Four Corners,\nreaching Montezuma Creek around 5 am MDT Sunday, cresting between\nBluff and Mexican Hat Sunday afternoon and peaking at Mexican Hat\nby 5 pm Sunday evening around 36 feet which will exceed all time\nrecord levels. The San Juan River will rise above flood stage\nSunday morning and continue to rise Sunday afternoon to moderate\nflood stage, peaking above major flood stage at Mexican Hat by 5\npm Sunday evening.\n- This includes the following streams and drainages...\nSan Juan River\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nMexican Hat, Tselakai Dezza, Bluff, Halchita, Montezuma Creek and\nAneth.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-10-26T13:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Montezuma, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.4ccb142ce710855634ed763bdfc50db7158ae6ba.001.1","ur